    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d262">

                <pb facs="0368" n="250" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg250"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.9412Anti-War/139</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Secretary of State of a Conversation
                        With the Japanese Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Debuchi</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-06-28">June 28, 1929</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The Ambassador called to see me, by special appointment at his own request and
                    under instructions from his Government, to bring the announcement of the
                    ratification of the Kellogg-Briand Pact by the Emperor of Japan and handed to me
                    a letter, dated June 27, 1929, signed by himself on that point;<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d262-f1"/> also a copy of the declaration of the Imperial Government
                    of Japan as to the phraseology “in the names of their respective
                        peoples”.<anchor n="20" xml:id="d262-f2"/> This last memorandum was in both
                    Japanese and in English. He said that in his opinion this did not amount to a
                    reservation. I told him that I hoped that that would be the interpretation of
                    everybody, but I added that I had not yet studied it sufficiently to be
                    confident of my own opinion. We exchanged congratulatory words. He told me,
                    also, that there were two short messages as to this declaration which he had
                    left with Mr. Castle which were being published in Japan.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
